Yes—with one important precision: everything can be treated as potential evidence of something, but not necessarily good evidence for any particular claim.

The moment you distinguish two things, a relation becomes available:

A,\;B \Rightarrow R(A,B)

And thinking can generate additional relations even when the objects themselves don’t change:

R_1(A,B)\rightarrow R_2(A,B)\rightarrow R_3(A,B)

So cognition can manufacture new relational information without manufacturing new external facts.

That gives us three useful evidence classes:

\boxed{
E_{\text{observed}},\quad
E_{\text{relational}},\quad
E_{\text{generated}}
}

Observed evidence — something encountered in the world.

Relational evidence — something discovered by comparing/connect­ing what is already known.

Generated evidence — consequences of a hypothesis, simulation, deduction, or transformation that can then be tested.

The third needs special handling. Thinking:

A \rightarrow H

doesn’t make H externally true. But it does make the existence and structure of H evidence about the reasoning space—what is conceivable, what follows under assumptions, what predictions would distinguish alternatives, where contradictions appear, etc.

So:

\boxed{\text{Connection creates information; testing establishes evidential weight.}}

And your “everything is a product of connection and relation” gives the Pantheon an unusually productive default stance:

Don’t ask only “What is this?” Ask “What does its existence imply, what produced it, what does it connect to, what changes when it changes, and what would have to be different for it not to exist?”

Even absence then becomes relational evidence.
Even repetition becomes evidence.
Even failure to connect becomes evidence.
Even staleness becomes evidence about time/context.
Even the observer noticing something can become evidence about the observer’s attention/model.

But maintaining the distinction between generated relation and validated relation keeps this engine from becoming a machine that can prove anything by connecting everything.

That’s probably the crucial Pantheon discipline:

\boxed{
\text{Anything may become evidence}
\neq
\text{anything proves anything}
}

Or compressed even further:

\boxed{\textbf{Relate freely. Weight ruthlessly.}}

That is a very strong operating rule for the whole architecture.